    "turn_text": "So the deal, so the deal, so the deal is that You know, the negotiations are still going on and the hope is that we can get to something. Nobody has given up hope. But look, here's the deal. You know, I think what the president wants is a bunch of money that he could hand out to his friends. And what we're saying is, yeah, we need the money, but we need to make sure it's directed to where it is needed most. We need to make sure that, you know, small businesses, not just the big super duper companies are getting relief. We need to keep our restaurants open. We need money for state and local governments. I mean, revenues are down because of businesses closing and shutdowns. And we don't want them to have to lay off essential employees like first responders. We need to make sure our hospitals are prepared because whether the president wants to admit it or not, the numbers are going up. in part because he's a terrible, terrible lousy example to this country. I mean, holding these outdoor rallies with, holding these rallies with no, people with no masks and, you know, basically downplaying the the impact of the virus. I mean, I've lost people in my constituents of mine, people I know. I've talked to, you know, husbands and wives and sons and daughters of people who suffer from this who died and who couldn't visit their loved one in the hospital. It's painful. And I, you know, this is not a joke. This is, this is not a, this is serious. And it really is, It's upsetting that he has so mismanaged this. I mean, his delay has been deadly. His mismanagement has been deadly. And it's criminal. And I have no more patience for his theatrics. I was in a supermarket in Worcester the other day. And a woman came up to me and I was wearing my John Lewis Good Trouble",
