  {
    "episode_id": 50996,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50996.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, no, you know, George McGovern and Joe Motely when I get sworn in, so during 1997, they sat with me on the house floor. Oh, boy. And I asked them both. I mean, what is the best advice you can give me to be a good member of Congress? And Joe Motely's advice, which was good advice, was get to know everybody on a first name basis. get to know where they have spouses, dogs, cats, canaries, kids, whatever. And start trying to build a personal relationship with them, because not everything you do here is a big ideological battle. I mean, say these people want to help you out because they like you. And I thought that was good advice. And George McGovern's advice to me was the same advice that was given to him when he first got elected to the Congress. And he said, you know, if you really want to be a good member of Congress, you have to get over the fear of losing an election. Because if all you're worried about is the next election, then you're going to just be a reflection of whatever the latest public opinion poll is. And you're not going to give people your best judgment. And I thought that was good advice too. I got to be honest with you, I don't think I'm quite over the fear of losing an election. But every time there's a tough vote, I kind of think about what is the right thing to do?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Not all legislative work involves major ideological battles.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "George McGovern and Joe Motley were present with me on the House floor in 1997.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "If a legislator focuses only on public opinion polls, they will not exercise independent judgment.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Joe Motley advised me to get to know my colleagues on a first-name basis and learn about their families and pets.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "I still experience fear about losing an election despite valuing independent judgment.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "George McGovern gave me the same advice he received upon his entry into Congress.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "In difficult votes, I reflect on what the morally correct action would be rather than polling data.",
