  {
    "episode_id": 50996,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50996.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 97,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, and here's the deal. Many people who are diabetics might not be diabetics if they wanted a different diet early on. I mean, you can prevent heart attacks with diet. I mean, there's so much. And over the years, we have great friends with Francis Collins, the head of NIH. And he's not moving NIH to focus more on this issue of food is medicine and trying to highlight that as well. And so I think this is the direction we're going. And it's moving slowly, but it's going in the right direction. And so I have a bill that I'm hoping to get you know, into one of the bigger bills that would set up a program within Medicare for medically tailored meals for patients who are released from hospitals. I mean, one of the things that we have is that one of the number one reasons why people who have major operations or severe treatments and they go home and up back in the hospital prematurely is lack of nutrition. They're weak. So the idea of giving them a bag of groceries and say, make your dinner, they don't. And so we can prescribe dietary specific meals",
