  {
    "episode_id": 50996,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50996.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 120,
    "turn_text": "Right. I wanted to pick up on a point that you had mentioned earlier about getting federal dollars back to your district. As chairman of the rules committee, and as somebody from my perspective, I enjoy watching these member day hearings and I enjoy watching sort of like the process of the rules in the house. I mean, you recently had a very sort of enlightening members day hearing where members are coming to say what they would like in terms of rules changes for the 117th Congress. And one issue that kept kind of coming up was this idea of congressionally directed spending also known as earmarks. How likely is that? And that's really for all intents and purposes. Tax paying dollars coming back to the taxpayers that you represent. I am very biased when it comes to legislative branch. I'm not necessarily Democrat or Republican, but legislative. Yes, all the way. I want my congressman. I want Don Baier to get as much money as he can for Arlington, Virginia. Thank you very much. And one of the ways to do that is through earmarks. How likely is it that earmarks will be coming back, do you think?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "We may not see earmarks return.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that congressional representatives should prioritize securing funding for their districts.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Earmarks are a legitimate and effective mechanism for directing federal funds to specific localities.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Taxpayers expect their elected officials to advocate for localized benefits from federal spending.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The legislative branch has primary responsibility for shaping how federal funds are allocated within Congress.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker identifies strongly with the role of the legislative branch over partisan affiliations.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "There is political value in advocating for constituency-specific appropriations even without direct party alignment.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Earmarks are perceived as a tool for accountability between representatives and their constituents.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts that members of Congress act in good faith when seeking targeted funding.",
        "confidence": 0.82
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 50996,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50996.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 121,
    "turn_text": "I hope they come back. I really do. I think one, as you mentioned, the idea that we're seeing all this power to the executive branch, no matter who the president is, doesn't make a lot of sense for me. It's almost as if we're all saying, well, I can't trust me to do what's right from my district. I mean, it's ridiculous. Yeah, you have to put a process in place as transparent with checks and balances. We could do that. And by the way, it's not just people like me who think we should bring it back. You heard on the Member's Day in the Rules Committee, Tom Cole, my Reiki member, Republican for Oklahoma, thinks it's a good idea. Yep. We were more likely to pass appropriations bills in a bipartisan way when people had skin in the game. And now appropriations bills have just become, depending on who's in power, a partisan vote. And you're like, this is not right. This is not",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I hope they come back.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The current concentration of power in the executive branch is problematic regardless of which party controls the presidency.",
