    "turn_idx": 139,
    "turn_text": "it. But the Rules Committee is like the traffic cop or Congress. I mean, all legislation that goes to the floor comes before the Rules Committee. Sometimes we have to make changes to the underlying text of the bill before it goes to the floor. We decide what amendments can be made in order, which ones we're not going to make in order, or whether we have a closed rule or a structured rule, whatever. So being on the Rules Committee, any member on the real screen, he has some influence in what any bill looks like before it goes to the house floor. So that's where a lot of the power comes from. So I have chairman all the time will say, just, no, it's like Alec Guinness in the bridge window of a choir. Don't touch my bridge, right? Perfect. Leave, right? You know, I want everything, right? And so it's this constant battle. I am proud of the fact that we have made more amendments in order than any of the Congress in history. And also, we've been able to figure out a way to make amendments in order during this pandemic when it takes on average of about an hour and a half to cast each vote. We've helped encourage on-block voting. So we can make dozens and dozens and dozens of amendments in order, but we would vote for them as an on-block rather than one at a time. I",
