  {
    "episode_id": 50996,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50996.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 175,
    "turn_text": "Because he was a role model. I mean, he, he, he was a decent, good man. I mean, you know, I loved him. I mean, he was the, he was the best. The reason why I'm on the roostomy, I got elected to Congress and we served together for a few years. And then in 2001, he was diagnosed with leukemia and he had, but it was complicated because he had been the recipient of a liver transplant. And he couldn't treat one without, in fact, so the doctor's basis is that you have three months to live. And so he called me and he said, look, this is the deal. I've got three months to live. But I need you to go on to the Rules Committee. And I'm like, well, I'm on the Transportation Committee and the Resources Committee. You're going to go on the Rules Committee. Tip O'Neill was on the Rules Committee before he ran for leadership. He put me on the Rules Committee. We need somebody from Massachusetts to be on the Rules Committee. And I remember he went, Dick Shepard had already promised that seat to the Congressional Black Caucus. Oh. And, you know, the next, the next available seat. So I said to him, I had read that in like roll call or something. He says, don't worry. So he called Dick Shepard up, who then was the minority leader. And he said, you know, Dick, I've got three months to live and I said, oh, I'm so sorry. Anything I could do if he just let me know, I'll do it. He said, just one, you name it, it's done. McGovern has to go on the rules committee. And so.",
