  {
    "episode_id": 50996,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50996.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 185,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I do. I mean, I look at it. I think sometimes sometimes I do. Sometimes I think, you know, it's like my way or the highway. And the deal is, look, I would like to win 100% of everything I want all the time. But I, you know, I can't even get 100% agreement with my wife. And so, I mean, like, If I can get 80%, boy, that's not bad. Or even 70%, or 65%. It all has to be perfect or nothing. And there's no such thing as a perfect bill. There's no such thing as a perfect congressman, a perfect politician. You know, I voted for one once in my life. That's when I first ran for office. And then, you know, I served two years and I ran for reelection. And I wasn't so perfect anymore. But I voted for me anyway, because I was better than the guy who was running against me. And that's one of the challenges right now, you know, is that we have some people who it is, you know, if you're not perfect in every way, then you're not a good member of Congress. I want to be as perfect as I can, but here's the deal. I didn't want to get something done. I'm not interested in 20 years from now, someone say, oh boy, he gave a speech and a perfect policy, but then never got anything done. I would rather have people say, this is what I aspire to do. I can't get it all now, but I moved the ball in the right direction. Because again, politics is supposed to be about helping people. And if it ceases to be that, then we have a problem. And I worry sometimes that the nature of our politics now is not focused as much on people as it is on winning and ambition and destroying people. And look, I know that sounds partisan, but I think Trump has so coursed our politics. I really worry that if there's not a change in November that I'm just I'm wary that we won't be able to turn this ship around.",
